.\" The manual page git reaches for when `git timebraid --help` is typed: git handles --help on a
.\" subcommand itself rather than passing it on, and looks for this file.
.\"
.\" Written, not generated. A manual page is allowed to word an option differently from --help, and
.\" mostly should -- it is read by someone who wants to understand the option, not to be reminded of
.\" it. What it may not do is describe an option the program does not have, or leave one out, so
.\" .github/scripts/check-man.py compares the two.
.\"
.\" That checker reads the line after each .TP in OPTIONS and holds it to one canonical spelling of
.\" a signature: names bold with their hyphens escaped and joined by `, `, the metavar italic after
.\" an unspaced `=`, and nothing else on the line. So
.\"
.\"     \fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR=\fI<path>\fR
.\"
.\" and not the .BR/.BI spellings, which say the same thing in ways the checker would have to guess
.\" at. Prose below the signature is free, and uses the macros.
.\"
.\" The version in .TH is the one being worked towards, as pom.xml carries it; the checker keeps
.\" the two equal, and open-next-version.sh moves it when it opens the next version.
.\"
.\" The date is that of the page's last nontrivial change, which is what man-pages(7) means by it
.\" and what a reader takes it for; the version bump open-next-version.sh makes is not one. Nothing
.\" holds it: a depth-1 CI checkout cannot read when a file last changed, so the check would have to
.\" be a full clone for one field. It is kept by hand.
.TH GIT\-TIMEBRAID 1 "2026\-09\-13" "git\-timebraid 0.2.0" "Git Extensions"
.
.SH NAME
git\-timebraid \- merge several git repositories into one, braided along the time axis
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.SY git\-timebraid
.OP \-o path
.OP options
.RI [ repo ]...
.YS
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
Merges several independent git repositories into one. Their commits are interleaved by timestamp,
so the result reads as a single history rather than as several histories stitched end to end, and
each input keeps its own subdirectory, or the root for the one placed there, in every tree that
holds it.
.PP
Nothing is written into the output until the whole plan is computed. A run whose plan cannot be
carried out says so before it has created the output, and
.B \-\-dry\-run
stops there deliberately. A remote input is cloned before that, beside the output, and the clone is
kept for the next run; a dry run without
.B \-o
clones into a temporary directory and removes it.
.PP
Every commit the run writes records where it came from, in a trailer naming the input repository and
the original commit and parents. That is what makes the result checkable rather than merely claimed:
a script can walk the output and confirm that no edge went missing.
.
.SS The repo argument
Each
.I repo
is written
.IR <path\-or\-url>[::[<subdir>][=<name>]] .
Everything before the last
.B ::
is the location and is used verbatim, never escaped, so a location holding a
.B ::
of its own needs a bare
.B ::
appended to say where it ends.
.PP
.I <subdir>
is where that input's content lands, and may be nested, as in
.BR ::libs/backend .
It defaults to
.IR <name> .
.PP
.I <name>
is the input's identity: the prefix on its recreated refs, the label in its provenance trailers, and
what
.B \-\-root\-repo
matches. It defaults to the last segment of
.IR <subdir> ,
or of the location. Neither field may be written with a
.B :
or a
.BR = .
.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Where the result is written
.TP
\fB\-o\fR, \fB\-\-output\fR=\fI<path>\fR
The repository to write. It must not exist yet or must be an empty directory;
.B \-\-force
also takes one that is not empty.
.TP
\fB\-\-force\fR
Write into a non-empty output directory instead of refusing it, over whatever it already holds: a
ref of a name the braid writes is replaced, and under \fB\-\-no\-bare\fR the checkout overwrites the
files at the braid's paths and removes those the output tracked that the braid does not have.
Untracked files are left as they were, and so is every ref the braid does not write, an earlier
run's included, but for those under \fBrefs/timebraid\-fetch/\fR, where a run parks what it
fetches. A directory holding an unrelated repository is a poor choice of output rather than a safe
one.
.
.SS Finding the inputs, and placing their content
.TP
\fB\-\-scan\fR=\fI<path>\fR
Take the layout from this directory instead of naming the inputs one by one. Every repository
beneath it becomes an input, placed in the output where it sits on disk, so the tree of clones you
already have is the tree the braid gets. A repository already found is not descended into, so what
it holds of its own \- a submodule, a vendored checkout \- is never a second input. A directory whose
name begins with a dot, and one reached through a symlink, are skipped along with anything under
them.
.TP
\fB\-\-root\-repo\fR=\fI<text>\fR
The input whose content lands at the output root rather than in a subdirectory of its own. Named by
identity, so it is the
.I <name>
of an input and not its path.
.TP
\fB\-\-splice\fR
Allow one input's subdirectory to lie inside another's, splicing the two into one directory. Without
it such a pair is refused, because a placement that buries one input inside another is more often a
mistake in the arguments than an intention.
.TP
\fB\-\-dissolve\-submodules\fR
Where an input lands exactly on a gitlink, replace that submodule with the input's own content. The
.B .gitmodules
section describing it is dropped along with it, so the output has the files rather than a pointer to
the repository they came from.
.
.SS Which history is read, and how it interleaves
.TP
\fB\-\-mainline\-branch\fR=\fI<branch>\fR
The branch treated as the mainline in every input. Every input contributed its own, and they
collapse into one branch at the braid's tip.
.IP
Repeatable. Prefixing a value with
.IB <input> ::
gives that one input a mainline of its own, and one quoted argument may hold several values
separated by spaces. An unscoped value covers the inputs not named that way, and is also the name
the output's branch takes; with none, the branch is named after the first input's mainline.
.IP
Defaults to the first of
.BR main ,
.B master
or
.B develop
that is present in every input.
.TP
\fB\-\-order\-by\fR=\fI(author|committer)\fR
Which of a commit's two timestamps decides where it lands among the commits of the other inputs.
Defaults to the committer date, which is the one that moves when a history is rewritten, and so the
one that reflects when the commit arrived where it now sits.
.
.SS Which refs a pattern speaks for
A
.I <ref\-pattern>
is
.IR [<input>::][^]<refspec> ,
matched against full ref names.
.IB <input> ::
narrows the pattern to one input, and a
.B ^
in front subtracts its matches instead of selecting them.
With nothing selected,
.B \-b
and
.B \-\-ref
subtract from every branch and tag, while
.B \-\-label\-ref
and
.BR \-\-interleave\-ref ,
which take no ref unless asked, refuse a
.B ^
alone.
.PP
The
.I <refspec>
is git's,
.IR <glob>[:<destination>] ,
and a value git takes as a refspec means the same here.
.BR \-b ,
.B \-\-ref
and
.B \-\-label\-ref
take the destination, saying where the matches land:
.B refs/heads/*:refs/tags/
writes every branch as a tag. Unlike git's fetch, a destination may name a namespace, as that one
does, left to the namespace's prefix; a glob with stars may go without a destination, which git
fetch allows only in a negative refspec, or name one ref as its destination; and there is no
.BR + ,
no empty glob or destination and no short name.
.PP
A destination substitutes
.B {repo}
for the input's name, and a
.B *
in it stands for what the glob's own star matched. A destination that names a ref outright, with
neither, gives every input the same output name and the prefixes do not apply \- which is what
makes two inputs meet, and what the refusal means when it says to give the destination a template
holding
.BR {repo} .
.PP
Every option in this section is repeatable, and one quoted argument may hold several patterns
separated by spaces.
.TP
\fB\-b\fR, \fB\-\-branch\fR=\fI<branch>\fR
Carry over these branches, by short name, or with
.B ^
leave them out. Shorthand for
.BI \-\-ref " refs/heads/<branch>" \fR,
and so subject to the same rule: naming one ref without a
.B ^
leaves out every ref not named, tags included. A run narrowed to a branch carries no tags unless it
says so as well.
.IP
The grammar above goes around the short name rather than inside it: a scope, a subtracting
.B ^
and a destination are held back, and only the branch is prefixed. So
.B \-b backend::^wip
says what
.B \-\-ref backend::^refs/heads/wip
says.
.TP
\fB\-\-ref\fR=\fI<ref\-pattern>\fR
Carry over the refs matching this pattern, branches and tags alike. Defaults to every branch and
every tag, each recreated in the namespace it came from.
.TP
\fB\-\-label\-ref\fR=\fI<ref\-pattern>\fR
Also recreate the refs matching this pattern, where the run already holds what they point at.
Adding one cannot change a commit the run writes, which a selection can: it names a ref without
letting it pull any history in. Defaults to none.
.TP
\fB\-\-interleave\-ref\fR=\fI<ref\-pattern>\fR
Let the commits on this ref delay a mainline merge that merges them in, rather than letting the
merge land by its own timestamp.
.B \&'refs/heads/* ^refs/heads/main'
is every side branch; a bare star opts in every tag as well. Defaults to none.
.
.SS What the output repository holds
.B \-\-tag\-prefix\fR,
.B \-\-branch\-prefix
and
.B \-\-notes\-prefix
qualify a ref name of the output. Each substitutes
.B {repo}
for the input's name, and an empty value qualifies nothing. Two inputs then meeting on one ref name
is refused, naming both, rather than resolved in favour of whichever was written last \- and the
same refusal applies where a pattern's destination spells a name out and takes the prefix off
entirely.
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]bare\fR
Write a bare output repository, which is the default.
.B \-\-no\-bare
checks out a working tree instead.
.TP
\fB\-\-keep\-remotes\fR
Add each input as a remote of the output, every ref it carried over landing under
.BR refs/remotes/<name>/* ,
at the original commits \- as does each input's mainline, whether the selection took it or not.
Notes are written under
.B refs/notes/
and are not mirrored. Those commits are in the output either way, since the run fills it by
fetching what every ref it read reaches; this is what keeps them one
.B git log
away instead of leaving them unreferenced.
.TP
\fB\-\-tag\-prefix\fR=\fI<text>\fR
The prefix on every recreated tag, so
.B v1.2
from
.B webui
becomes
.BR webui/v1.2 .
Defaults to
.BR {repo}/ .
.TP
\fB\-\-branch\-prefix\fR=\fI<text>\fR
The prefix on every recreated branch, the mainline aside \- that one collapses into a single
unprefixed branch. Defaults to
.BR {repo}/ .
.TP
\fB\-\-subject\-prefix\fR=\fI<text>\fR
The prefix on every commit subject, so a subject from
.B webui
reads
.BR "webui: fix the date picker" .
Substitutes
.B {repo}
and
.BR {subdir} ,
the input's name and its destination. Defaults to
.B "\(dq{repo}: \(dq"
\- the quotes mark the trailing space rather than being part of it.
.TP
\fB\-\-notes\fR
Read every input's
.B refs/notes/
and carry the notes over, rekeyed onto the commits this run writes \- a note is filed under the sha
of the object it annotates, and every commit here has a new one. A note on an object the run did not
write is skipped, and the run reports how many. Notes are not read at all unless this is given.
.TP
\fB\-\-notes\-prefix\fR=\fI<text>\fR
The prefix on every recreated notes ref, below
.BR refs/notes/ .
An input with notes usually has
.BR refs/notes/commits ,
which is what
.BR git\-notes (1)
writes by default, so with an empty prefix two inputs meeting on that name is refused, naming both,
rather than resolved.
Defaults to
.BR {repo}/ .
.TP
\fB\-\-lightweight\-tags\fR
Recreate every annotated tag as a lightweight one, writing no tag object and dropping its tagger,
date and message. It asks for that loss deliberately. Without it an annotated tag stays annotated.
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]provenance\fR
Record each commit's original sha and parents in a trailer on its message. On by default;
.B \-\-no\-provenance
gives up the record and with it the ability to check the result.
.TP
\fB\-\-provenance\-trailer\fR=\fI<text>\fR
The trailer
.B \-\-provenance
writes, as its own paragraph. Substitutes
.BR {repo} ,
.B {commit}
and
.BR {parents} ;
a template leaving out the last two keeps the trailer and gives up what makes the result checkable,
since those are what a checking script reads. Defaults to:
.RS 7
.EX
[timebraid: repo="{repo}" commit={commit} parents={parents}]
.EE
.RE
.
.SS Inspecting a run
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Compute the plan, summarize it, and write no output.
.TP
\fB\-\-plan\-out\fR=\fI<path>\fR
Dump the deterministic plan as text to this file. It is the commit-by-commit account that
.B \-\-verbose
cannot give, writing the commits being the one step that is not a git command.
.TP
\fB\-q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Say nothing but the closing report and any error: no phase headings, no bars. Refused together with
.BR \-\-verbose .
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Print the git command behind each step: each one it shells out to, and the equivalent of the object
transfer and of every ref written.
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]progress\fR
Draw progress, or refuse to, whatever standard error happens to be \- for a log of a run that is
taking too long, or a terminal to keep clean. Drawn by default when standard error is a terminal,
and not otherwise.
.IP
These are two options and not one switch, unlike
.BR \-\-[no\-]bare
and
.BR \-\-[no\-]provenance :
giving both is an error, as is
.B \-\-progress
together with
.BR \-\-quiet .
.TP
\fB\-\-[no\-]ascii\fR
Draw progress with ASCII characters only, or with the full set, for a console that shows the bar as
question marks or one that was misread the other way. Defaults to whichever of the two the console
can encode. Two options rather than one switch, so giving both is an error.
.
.SS Getting help
.TP
\fB\-h\fR
Show each option's first line and exit. Every option is there; what is left out is the qualifiers
and the defaults beneath each first line, and all but the first paragraph of the text above the
options and above each group of them.
.TP
\fB\-hh\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Show every option with its defaults and exit.
.B \-hh
is the spelling that reaches the program under either name: run as
.BR "git timebraid" ,
.B \-\-help
is handled by git itself and prints this page.
.TP
\fB\-\-version\fR
Show the version and exit.
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The braid was written, or the run was asked only to describe itself.
.TP
.B 1
The run was refused or failed. A refusal of the plan happens before anything is written into the
output; one of a ref name, or a failure part way, leaves the output where it got to, which is why
the output has to be new or empty unless
.B \-\-force
says otherwise.
.PP
Reached as
.BR "git timebraid \-\-help" ,
the status is neither of these: it belongs to whatever git handed the page to.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TIMEBRAID_JAVA
The
.B java
binary to run, overriding the launcher's own search for one.
.TP
.B JAVA_HOME
The JVM run where the archive carries no runtime of its own and
.B TIMEBRAID_JAVA
is not set; without either, the
.B java
on
.BR PATH .
.TP
.B JAVA_OPTS
Passed to that JVM. Typically a larger heap for a large graph, as in
.BR JAVA_OPTS=\-Xmx4g .
.TP
.B COLUMNS
The width
.B \-h
and
.B \-hh
lay out to, in place of what the terminal reports. A run's own phase headings are ruled to it as
well, so it decides how wide the output of a merge is and not only that of the help.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
Merge two bare clones, each into a subdirectory named after it:
.PP
.RS 7
.EX
git timebraid \-o /tmp/merged \e
    \-\-mainline\-branch develop \e
    ~/repos/backend.git ~/repos/webui.git
.EE
.RE
.PP
Group the inputs under a layout of your own, two of them sharing
.BR libs/ :
.PP
.RS 7
.EX
git timebraid \-o /tmp/merged \e
    ~/repos/backend.git::libs/backend \e
    ~/repos/codegen.git::libs/codegen \e
    ~/repos/webui.git::apps/webui
.EE
.RE
.PP
Put
.B backend
at the root, and give
.B webui
the subdirectory
.B ui
without letting that rename it, so its tags stay
.BR webui/v1.2 :
.PP
.RS 7
.EX
git timebraid \-o /tmp/merged \e
    \-\-root\-repo backend \-\-mainline\-branch main \e
    ~/repos/backend.git ~/repos/webui.git::ui=webui
.EE
.RE
.PP
Carry over two branches and one release series of tags, and inspect the
plan without writing the output repository:
.PP
.RS 7
.EX
git timebraid \-\-dry\-run \-\-plan\-out /tmp/plan.txt \e
    \-b main \-b release/2.2 \-\-ref 'refs/tags/v2.*' \e
    ~/repos/backend.git ~/repos/webui.git
.EE
.RE
.
